Derive the following Thermodynamic Potentials and Natural Variables relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\begin{aligned}
dH&=dU+p\,dV+V\,dp\\
&=T\,dS+V\,dp+\sum_i\mu_i\,dN_i,
\end{aligned}
\qquad H=H(S,p,\{N_i\}).
\tag*{[3]}
\]
Notation: \(U,H,F,G\) denotes internal energy, enthalpy, Helmholtz free energy, and Gibbs free energy; unit \(\mathrm{J}\); \(S,T,p,V\) denotes entropy, absolute temperature, pressure, and total volume; unit \(\mathrm{J K^{-1}, K, Pa, m^{3}}\); \(N_i,\mu_i,i,j\) denotes particle number, particle chemical potential, selected species label, and other species label; unit \(\mathrm{1, J, 1, 1}\); \(F_0,a,V_0\) denotes reference Helmholtz energy, constant in the example model, and reference volume; unit \(\mathrm{J, J K^{-1}, m^{3}}\); \(d\) denotes exact differential of a state function.

